eatured artist just so we don't  have a ton of duplicate rows  now like i mentioned we want to end up  with just song column  and artist column with all of the artist  names and then  another column let's say artist type  that tells us if they were a featured  artist  or a primary artist so we'll call pivot  longer  and we have to specify the columns that  we want to pivot on and we can do this  by indexing the column numbers so we're  going to do 2 to 3 which is the second  and third column we also have to pass in  a names2 parameter which is either going  to be primary artist or featured artist  so we'll call this artist type and we  also have to specify the column name  for the values so we'll just do artist  name  so if i go ahead and run this now we'll  have all of our artist names  contained in just one column and if  there is no featured artist it's going  to impute the n  a and if you're wondering why this might  be useful well let's filter for an  artist  and let's actually check out drake and  we can see that there's some songs where  he's a featured artist  and there's some songs where he's a  primary artist and theoretically going  back to our old data frame we could have  done a filter to check if  primary artist was drake or a featured  artist was drake  but having all of our artists in just  one column can be useful for other types  of analysis  for instance i can really easily figure  out how many times he was a featured  artist and how many times he was  primary artist just by counting on the  artist type  and we can see in all the distinct songs  he was a primary artist 135 times and  featured 48 times  but just a quick note if there's any  situation where there  are multiple featured artists and drake  is one of them then obviously this  filter condition won't match  so it would be excluding that data we're  not going to fix it now just because  this is a tutorial but that's just  something to keep in mind  so pivot wider essentially does the  opposite of pivot longer  it takes one column with a different set  of values and then makes those different  values into different columns  so for example let's just filter for the  songs that have ranked in the top three  spots  using that rank column that we have so  we'll filter where rank  is less than or equal to three and we'll  select  the date the rank and the song and just  take a look at that  so pivot wider as the name suggests can  make our data frame wider so right now  we have  rank contained in just one column but  what if i wanted to have  for each date a rank 1 a rank 2 and a  rank 3 column well that's when i'd use  pivot wider  so it's pretty simple to use we'll just  call pivot wider and now we have to pass  in  names from which is going to be the  column that we want to essentially make  wider  and that's going to be rank and then  we're going to pass in a values from  parameter  which is going to be the actual values  it'll impute into those columns  and that's going to be from our song so  if we run that now we have for each date  the rank 1 song in one column the rank 2  song  in another column and then the third  ranking song in the last column  now let's move on to merging some data  frames merging data frames is  definitely something you'll likely do in  r and it's really not too difficult with  dplyr so i found another data set online  of 10 000  mtv's top music artists and i'll put a  link in the description but let's check  out our data set  we've got the name of the artist some  social media links  and then the genre of the artist i don't  really care too much about the social  media links but i think it'd be useful  to connect  the artists in our billboard 100 data  frame with the artists in this data  frame  that way we can get the genre of the  music and maybe even see what the most  popular genres are  so before we start merging and joining  our data let's talk about the different  types of joins that there are  because it'll affect what your merge  data set looks like first is the inner  join  or just the join which will only keep  the rows of each table where there's a  match  so if there's a data point in the first  table that doesn't exist in the second  table  that row won't appear in our inner join  data set next is the left join or left  outer join which will keep all the rows  in the first table  but only the rows that match the  condition in the second table  so your merge data set will still have  all the rows of the first table  and then impute null values in the  columns of the second table if there's  no match  the right join or right outer join is  essentially the same thing as a left  join but it retains all the rows in the  second table and only keeps the rows in  the first table that match the condition  and then the full join or full outer  join retains all rows in both tables  i'm going to do an inner join here which  will drop all the rows in both the  billboard 100 and mtv data frames where  there's no match  so this means that any analysis we do  now will ignore potentially a lot of  data points and could give you  inaccurate results but we'll come back  to this in a second  so i've grabbed my music df clean data  set and i'll call that inner join  command  and we've already implicitly passed in  musicdf.cleaned as our first argument to  interjoin and we want to join it to the  mtv data  we also need to specify what column we  want to join on  so let's take a look at our column names  in musicdf.cleaned we want to join on  the primary artist  and on mtv data we want to join on the  name column  so we'll specify by which is going to be  how we're joining our data  and we're going to say primary artist  which is the column in the music the  updater frame  is going to be equal to the name which  is going to be the artist column in mtv  data  and just to see if our rejoin worked  we're going to select our song  our primary artist and the genre and now  the song which comes from our music df  data frame  has a genre attached to it which comes  from the mtv data frame  so if we want to quickly do some  analysis to see what genres are most  popular in the billboard hot 100  let's filter out all of the n a rows for  genre  and look at our distinct rows so if  there were multiple songs that were in  the billboard hot 100 now we're just  looking at  each individual one and now we want to  count on the genre name  so we can see that there are 49  different genres and then if we want to  grab the top 15  we can just call top end 15 and now just  getting this in descending order  we'll arrange it with descending by  n and now we can see in order the most  popular genre is by how many  songs they have of that genre i think  it'd be interesting to see if there's  any visible trend in popularity of these  genres  over the last decade we can actually  really easily pipe our data into the  graph functions with ggplot2 so let me  load in our ggplot2 library  and instead of seeing how all these  genres vary over time  i just want to look at the top 5. so we  can take the same query that we wrote  before  i'm going to change this to 5 and i just  want to get these genre names  into a vector so i don't really need  this line to arrange it  but i am going to call the pull command  on genre and this will just get me a  vector  with the top five genre names and we can  use this when making our graph  let me just save this as top five genres  and set it equal  and let's come down here to where we  want to make our graph and  i'll copy a bit of this code maybe up to  here  so this is just joining our two data  sets together and now let's select  our date our song in our genre we can  see what this looks like  and now we want to filter for the rows  where this genre is in  our top five genre so we'll call filter  where  genre is in top 5  genres and initially this has 35 000  rows  but if i run this we go down to 23 000  rows now i just want to see how many  songs of each genre are there for each  date  so i want to use that count function and  will group by the date  and the genre and now we can see on the  first week for billboard hot 100 of 2011  there were two contemporary country  songs 19 country  to hip hop and so on and so forth but i  do have a feeling that if we graph this  data it's going to be quite messy to  read  and we should probably aggregate this by  either month or year  first we'll take a look at what the data  looks like so we'll pipe this into  ggplot we're going to set our aesthetic  where the x-axis is going to be our date  the y-axis  is going to be the n value so the count  and then  we're going to pass in color just so we  can distinguish the different lines  and each line is going to be based on  their genre so we'll pass in genre  and then we just need to add the geom  line layer  and we can take a look let's zoom out a  bit  so this data looks pretty messy i think  it'd be better if we  aggregated the data by either month or  year so let's try doing that  we're just going to create a new column  called date that's going to overwrite  our previous state similar to what we  did before and we're going to call the  for date function  and let's pass in date which is the  column that we're going to floor by and  then the unit  which let's try a year and then we'll  pipe this into the rest of our data  so again this will take anything that  happened in let's say 2011  whether it's in january or february or  december  and it'll make it all january 1st of  2011  and then for 2012 it'll make everything  that happen in 2012  into january 1st of 2012 and this is  just so we can group our data by the  years  so if we run all of this now our data is  a bit smoother  and a little easier to interpret so just  based off this graph it looks like most  of these genres have decreased in  popularity over time  especially pop and country so it's  possible that some of the other genres  became more popular but what's also  possible  is that when we did an inner join with  our mtv dataset we might have  unintentionally dropped a lot of rows  for more recent years and this would  certainly be the case if the billboard  hot 100 artists  from the last few years weren't in the  mtv data set to begin with  so we'd need to further investigate if  other genres are becoming more popular  while the top five are becoming less  popular or if we just have a lack of  data these were some advanced deep  player concepts i felt were worth  covering  but if you do any kind of data wrangling  or data cleaning i'm sure you'll be  using at least one of these packages  thanks for watching and i'll catch you  in the next one
 

